From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] libmemtrack: a device without the HAL reports no extra memory

memtrack reports the memory of a process that /proc/pid/smaps does not account
for, typically a GPU's own. This device has no such memory: the only graphics
buffers are gralloc's, which are ashmem and appear in the maps of every process
that holds them.

With no memtrack HAL, libmemtrack logged "Couldn't load memtrack module" on
every call (the service is looked up once, the message was not) and answered
every caller with -1, which made system_server log "failed to get memory
consumption info: -1" for each process it measured: 110 and 110 lines in one
session (PW3 Android, Test V48). Dumps of memory were otherwise complete, as
nothing was missing from the maps.

It now says this once, at the lookup, and reports no records instead of an
error. A HAL that is there and fails still returns an error.

--- a/libmemtrack/memtrack.cpp
+++ b/libmemtrack/memtrack.cpp
@@ -45,10 +45,17 @@
 
 //TODO(b/31632518)
 static android::sp<IMemtrack> get_instance() {
-    static android::sp<IMemtrack> module = IMemtrack::getService();
-    if (module == nullptr) {
-        ALOGE("Couldn't load memtrack module");
-    }
+    /* The service is looked up once; the message was not, so a device without
+     * the HAL logged an error for every process measured. Say it once, and as
+     * what it is: memtrack reports the memory that /proc/pid/smaps does not
+     * account for, and a device whose only graphics buffers are ashmem has
+     * none of it. */
+    static android::sp<IMemtrack> module = [] {
+        android::sp<IMemtrack> m = IMemtrack::getService();
+        if (m == nullptr)
+            ALOGI("No memtrack HAL: all memory of a process is in its maps");
+        return m;
+    }();
     return module;
 }
 
@@ -67,8 +74,11 @@
 {
     int err = 0;
     android::sp<IMemtrack> memtrack = get_instance();
-    if (memtrack == nullptr)
-        return -1;
+    if (memtrack == nullptr) {
+        /* No HAL: no records to add, which is the answer, not a failure. */
+        t->records.resize(0);
+        return 0;
+    }
 
     Return<void> ret = memtrack->getMemory(pid, type,
         [&t, &err](MemtrackStatus status, hidl_vec<MemtrackRecord> records) {
